course: dictionary
lesson: 15241
title: Sheath: a cover that holds something safe
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A sheath is a close-fitting cover. Its main job is to hold something sharp or delicate and keep it protected. Say it as «sheeth», and the plural «sheaths» as «sheeths». «The knight pulled his sword from its sheath.» The sheath is the cover the sword slides into, so a sheath is like a case for a blade.
explain: You can use context clues to find the meaning. «Always keep the kitchen knife in its sheath when you carry it.» The word «carry» and the idea of keeping something safe tell you a sheath covers a blade. In science, a sheath can be a wrapping layer around a part of a plant or animal: «The leaf sheath wraps around the stem of the grass.»
explain: The verb is «sheathe», which means to put a blade back into its cover. «She sheathed her sword after the battle.» Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «She put her sword back into its cover after the battle.» It still makes sense, so you have understood the word.
fact: A sheath is a close-fitting cover, often for a knife, sword or dagger.
fact: «The knight pulled his sword from its sheath.» Here the sheath holds the blade.
fact: In science, a sheath is a covering around part of a plant or animal, such as the «myelin sheath» that protects a nerve.
fact: A sheath can also be a protective covering around a cable or wire.
fact: «Sheathe» is the verb: «She sheathed her sword» means she put it back into its cover.
exercise: «The knight pulled his sword out of its sheath.» What is a sheath? | A loud battle cry | A horse's saddle | A type of castle wall | *A close-fitting cover for a blade
exercise: Mum says, «Keep the knife in its sheath when you carry it.» Why does she say this? | To make the knife heavier | To make the knife shine | *To cover the blade and keep people safe | To hide the knife in the sea
exercise: «The leaf sheath wraps around the stem of the grass.» What does the sheath do? | It turns the grass brown | It lets the grass swim | *It wraps around and protects the stem | It cuts the grass
test: Which word means a close-fitting cover for a blade? | Edge | Handle | *Sheath | Point
test: «She sheathed her sword after the battle.» What does sheathed mean? | *Put the sword back into its cover | Shouted loudly | Sharpened the blade | Dropped the sword on the ground
exam: Which sentence uses sheath to mean a simple, close-fitting dress? | The myelin sheath helps nerve messages travel fast | She put the knife back in its sheath | *She wore a plain green sheath to the party | The leaves formed a sheath around the stem
